.\" OTPme ACL reference.
.\"
.\" This file is generated by update_acls_man.py. Do not edit it: the next
.\" run overwrites it. The ACLs come from the classes themselves, so
.\" change the ACL lists in the code and run the script again.
.TH OTPME\-ACLS 7 "2026-10-23" "OTPme 0.3.0a286" "OTPme Manual"
.SH NAME
otpme\-acls \- the ACLs of the OTPme object types
.SH DESCRIPTION
What an ACL is, how it is written and how it is passed on is in
.BR otpme (7),
section
.BR "ACCESS CONTROL" .
This page is the list: which ACLs each kind of object knows.
.PP
An object tells you the same itself.
.B list_acls
shows the ACLs an object has, and after
.B add_acl
the shell completion offers the ones it takes:
.RS
.B otpme\-user list_acls \fIalice\fR
.RE
.PP
This page is generated from the code, so it is what this version supports.
.SH HOW TO READ THIS PAGE
A permission is the heading, what it applies to stands under it.
Under
.B view
the entry
.B tokens
means the ACL
.BR view:tokens .
Under
.B Alone
stand the permissions that take nothing after them.
.PP
A permission without a value covers all of its values:
.B edit
allows every
.BR edit:x .
.B all
allows everything.
.B view_all
covers
.B view
and
.BR view_public ,
and
.B view
covers
.BR view_public .
.PP
Two entries stand for a list of their own:
.TP
.B attribute:<name>
One ACL per LDAP attribute of the object, for example
.BR edit:attribute:mail .
Which attributes an object has depends on the extensions it has.
The permission without the attribute (\fBedit:attribute\fR) covers all of them.
.TP
.B config:<parameter>
One ACL per config parameter of that object type, for example
.BR set:config:allow_temp_passwords .
.B show_config
lists the parameters of an object.
The permission without the parameter (\fBset:config\fR) covers all of them.
.SH THE ACLS OF EVERY OBJECT
Every kind of object has these.
The list of an object type below names only what it has on top of them.
.TP
.B add
Every add:x of the object.
.TP
.B all
Everything. Every permission of the object, also the ones a later version adds.
.TP
.B delete
Every delete:x of the object, and so the object itself.
.TP
.B disable
Every disable:x of the object.
.TP
.B edit
Every edit:x of the object.
.TP
.B enable
Every enable:x of the object.
.TP
.B export
Write the object out (export).
.TP
.B import
Reserved. Reading an object in needs admin rights, the ACL is not asked for.
.TP
.B move
Move the object to another unit.
.TP
.B remove
Every remove:x of the object.
.TP
.B rename
Rename the object, the same as rename:object.
.TP
.B set
Every set:x of the object.
.TP
.B touch
Write the object again without changing it. Builds its index, its ACLs and its LDIF again.
.TP
.B verify_signature
Check the signatures of the object. Its own permission, so it can be given without view.
.TP
.B view
The normal data of an object. Covers view_public.
.TP
.B view_all
All data, also the private ones like a certificate key. Covers view and view_public.
.TP
.B view_public
The public data of an object, what LDAP or getent(1) show without a login.
.TP
.B add:acl
Add an ACL (add_acl).
.TP
.B add:attribute
Add an LDIF attribute.
.TP
.B add:extension
Add an extension, which is what brings the LDAP attributes.
.TP
.B assign:policy
Assign a policy.
.TP
.B clear:changelog
Empty the changelog. Needs the config parameter allow_changelog_clearing as well.
.TP
.B delete:acl
Remove an ACL (del_acl).
.TP
.B delete:attribute
Remove an LDIF attribute.
.TP
.B delete:object
Delete the object.
.TP
.B disable:acl_inheritance
Stop the object from taking the default ACLs of its unit.
.TP
.B edit:attribute
Change an LDIF attribute.
.TP
.B edit:changelog
Change the comment of a changelog entry.
.TP
.B edit:description
Change the description.
.TP
.B edit:info
Change the free text info of the object.
.TP
.B enable:acl_inheritance
Let the object take the default ACLs of its unit again.
.TP
.B remove:extension
Remove an extension.
.TP
.B remove:orphans
Remove references to objects that are gone.
.TP
.B remove:policy
Remove a policy. A plain remove does not cover it.
.TP
.B rename:object
Rename the object.
.TP
.B set:config
Change every config parameter of the object.
.TP
.B set:config:<parameter>
Change one config parameter.
.TP
.B view:acl_inheritance
See whether the object takes the default ACLs of its unit.
.TP
.B view:attribute
See the LDIF attributes.
.TP
.B view:changelog
See the changelog.
.TP
.B view:checksum
See the checksum of the object.
.TP
.B view:config
See every config parameter.
.TP
.B view:config:<parameter>
See one config parameter.
.TP
.B view:create_time
See when the object was created.
.TP
.B view:creator
See who created it.
.TP
.B view:description
See the description.
.TP
.B view:extension
See the extensions of the object.
.TP
.B view:full_oid
See the full OID, with realm and site.
.TP
.B view:info
See the free text info.
.TP
.B view:last_modified
See when it was changed last.
.TP
.B view:last_used
See when it was used last.
.TP
.B view:object
See the object at all. Without it the object is not there for the token.
.TP
.B view:path
See the path in the tree.
.TP
.B view:policies
See the policies of the object.
.TP
.B view:read_oid
See the short OID.
.TP
.B view:rel_path
See the path below the site.
.TP
.B view:resolver
See which resolver the object comes from.
.TP
.B view:resolver_checksum
See the checksum of the resolver data.
.TP
.B view:resolver_key
See the key the resolver knows it by.
.TP
.B view:sync_checksum
See the checksum the sync works with.
.TP
.B view:uuid
See the UUID.
